course: biology
lesson: 5
title: The desert: living with little water
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A desert is a place with very little rain, not necessarily a hot one: deserts bake by day but can freeze at night, because dry air and clear skies let the ground's heat escape into space once the sun goes down.
explain: Every desert living thing is a water saver: the cactus stores it, the kangaroo rat makes it from its food, the beetle catches it from fog and the flowers wait years as seeds for one good rain.
explain: Animals also dodge the heat: they hide in burrows by day, come out at night, lift their feet off scorching sand or carry big ears that give off heat like radiators.
explain: In the Road Runner cartoons the bird says «meep meep» and leaves Wile E. Coyote in a cloud of dust; a real roadrunner runs at about 30 km per hour and a real coyote can run about twice as fast, so coyotes do sometimes catch roadrunners.
explain: At the zoo a camel kneels with its humps wobbling, and many people think the humps are water tanks; they are actually stores of fat, and a dromedary has one hump while a Bactrian camel has two.
fact: A camel's hump stores fat, not water; keeping fat in one place lets heat escape from the rest of the body, and a thirsty camel can drink more than 100 litres in about 10 minutes.
fact: The fennec fox of the Sahara is the smallest fox, with the biggest ears for its size, which give off heat and hear prey under the sand; furry soles protect its feet from hot sand.
fact: The saguaro cactus of Arizona and Mexico can grow over 12 metres tall and live over 150 years; its pleated stem swells like an accordion to store water, and its first arm often grows only at about 50 to 70 years old.
fact: Desert sand can be hotter than 60 degrees Celsius, so some lizards lift their feet in turn to cool them, and many animals dive into the cooler sand a few centimetres down.
fact: Namib fog-basking beetles stand head-down in the morning sea fog so that drops collect on their backs and run down to their mouths.
fact: The sidewinder rattlesnake moves sideways, touching the hot sand at only two points and leaving tracks like a row of letter J's; it is venomous.
fact: Desert seeds can wait in the soil for years and, after heavy rain, sprout, flower and seed within a few weeks, sometimes as a superbloom even in the Atacama.
fact: Kangaroo rats can live without ever drinking: they get water from dry seeds and from burning their food, make very concentrated urine and plug their cool burrows by day.
fact: Scorpions glow blue-green under ultraviolet light, and scientists are still not sure why; of more than 2,000 kinds, only a few dozen have venom dangerous to people, but none should be picked up.
fact: The Australian thorny devil eats thousands of small ants at a meal, draws water from damp sand through grooves in its skin to its mouth and shows a spiny false head to predators.
fact: The greater roadrunner is a cuckoo that runs at about 30 km per hour, eats lizards, insects and even rattlesnakes, and warms up on cold mornings by showing dark skin to the sun.
fact: A coyote can run at about 65 km per hour, and coyotes are among the real predators of roadrunners.
fact: Deserts are defined by very little rain, not by heat, so they can drop below freezing at night, and Antarctica is the biggest desert on Earth.
exercise: What is inside a camel's hump? | Fat, which the camel can live on when food is scarce | Water, which the camel sips slowly whenever it gets thirsty on a long trip | Air, which helps the camel float | Bones that hold up the saddle
exercise: How many humps has a dromedary camel? | One | Two | Three | None
exercise: Why does a fennec fox have such huge ears? | To give off heat and to hear prey under the sand | To scare other foxes away from its burrow at night | To shade its eyes from the bright desert sun all day | To dig its burrows much faster in the soft, loose sand
exercise: How does a saguaro cactus store water? | Its pleated stem swells like an accordion after rain | It keeps water in its big, flat, shiny leaves | It stores water in a pond around its roots, under the sand | It carries water in its spines
exercise: About how old is a saguaro when it grows its first arm? | About 50 to 70 years | Only about 1 year old | Only about 5 years old | It is born with all its arms
exercise: Why do some desert lizards lift their feet one after another? | The sand is so hot that each foot needs a moment to cool | They are dancing to attract a mate | They are stamping hard on the sand to scare away the snakes hiding below | They are feeling for water with their toes
exercise: How does the Namib fog-basking beetle get water? | Fog drops collect on its back and run down to its mouth | It digs down to an underground lake far beneath the dunes | It drinks from cactus flowers | It never needs any water at all
exercise: Why does a sidewinder move sideways across the sand? | Less of its body touches the hot, slippery sand | It cannot see where it is going in the bright sun | Its head is far too heavy for it to go straight forwards | It is hiding its tracks from eagles flying overhead
exercise: What happens when heavy rain falls on a desert? | Seeds that waited for years sprout and flower within weeks | The desert turns into a forest for ever, with tall trees and rivers | All the plants drown and rot | Nothing, desert seeds are all dead
exercise: How does a kangaroo rat live without drinking? | It gets water from seeds and from burning its food, and saves it carefully | It licks dew from cactus spines each morning | It drinks from puddles at night, when there is nobody about to watch it or catch it | It stores water in its long tail like a camel's hump
exercise: What do scorpions do under ultraviolet light? | They glow blue-green | They become invisible | They glow a bright red | They fall asleep at once
exercise: How dangerous are most scorpions to people? | Only a few dozen kinds have venom dangerous to people | Every scorpion can kill a person with a single sting | No scorpion can hurt people at all | Only scorpions that glow under a torch are dangerous to people
exercise: How does a thorny devil drink? | Grooves in its skin draw water from damp sand to its mouth | It drinks from rivers with its long tongue, like a cat | It never needs water | It sucks water from the bodies of the ants it eats, and nothing else
exercise: What does a thorny devil show a predator? | A spiny false head on its neck | A bright red mouth | A puff of poisonous smoke | Its long sticky tongue
exercise: What kind of bird is a roadrunner? | A cuckoo that prefers running to flying | A small kind of ostrich that lives in America | A small eagle that hunts on foot in the desert | A kind of wild desert chicken that cannot fly
exercise: Why can a desert night be freezing after a baking day? | Dry air and clear skies let the heat escape into space | The sun goes to the other side of the desert | The sand makes cold air at night | Cold wind blows in from the sea every night, even far inland
exercise: What makes a place a desert? | Very little rain, whether hot or cold | Very hot weather all year round | Lots of sand dunes | No animals or plants at all
test: «road runner's well faster than the coyote, cartoon proves it» What is right? | A real coyote runs about twice as fast and sometimes catches roadrunners | Yes, roadrunners run at 300 km per hour, faster than any car on the road | Yes, coyotes can never catch them | No, roadrunners can fly away at jet speed
test: «camels store water in the humps so they don't need to drink» What is right? | No, the humps are fat, though a camel can drink over 100 litres at once | Yes, each hump is a water tank | Yes, and they refill the humps from rain that falls on their backs in the desert | No, the humps are full of air
test: Count them: two Bactrian camels and three dromedaries at the zoo. How many humps? | 7 | 5 | 10 | 6
test: Count them: a scorpion has 8 legs and 2 pincers. How many legs on four scorpions? | 32 | 40 | 24 | 16
test: «found a glowing scorpion with me UV torch, gonna grab it for a pic» What is the best advice? | Do not pick it up, even though most kinds are not deadly | Grab it, glowing ones are safe | Grab it by the tail, because it can't sting you if you hold it there | Fine, scorpions only sting in the day
test: «desert at night, why's it freezing, we're in the Sahara ffs» Which is the best answer? | Dry air and clear skies let the ground's heat escape into space | The Sahara has winter every night | Desert sand makes ice at night | Cold comes up from deep under the sand when the sun goes down
test: What would happen to desert flowers if it never rained heavily for ten years? | Their seeds could wait in the soil until a good rain came | They would grow anyway without water | They would all turn into cactuses | The seeds would sprout every year in the dry sand and flower in the heat
test: «antarctica's a desert?? it's ice not sand, you're mad» Which is the best answer? | A desert means very little rain or snow, and Antarctica gets very little | It is a desert only in summer | It is not a desert, because deserts must always be hot and covered in sand | It is a desert because it has sand under the ice
test: «a rat that never drinks? rubbish» Which is the best answer? | True: the kangaroo rat gets water from its food and wastes almost none | True, but only because it drinks blood | Rubbish, every rat must drink water every single day, or it will die within a week | True, because it stores water in its cheeks
test: «that cactus is mega tall, must be like 10 years old» What is right? | A tall saguaro is probably over 100 years old, they grow very slowly | Yes, cactuses grow a metre a year, so it is about ten | It is probably 1 year old | Cactuses never get older than 10
test: «why's the snake going sideways, is it broken or what» Which is the best answer? | Sidewinding touches the hot sand at only two points and does not slip | The snake is injured and limping | It is lost and looking for home | It always moves sideways, even in water, because it cannot go forwards
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the contents of the hump of a camel? | The hump constitutes a reserve of fat upon which the animal may subsist when nourishment is scarce | The hump constitutes a reservoir of water from which the animal drinks in the course of a long journey | The hump constitutes a cavity filled with air | The hump constitutes a mass of bone
exam: Identify the principal reason for which desert temperatures may decline below freezing during the night. | In the absence of atmospheric moisture and cloud, the heat of the ground escapes freely into space | The Sun is positioned nearer to the Earth by day | The sand generates cold during the hours of darkness | Glacial winds traverse every desert nightly from the polar regions and from the mountains of the interior
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the kangaroo rat. | The kangaroo rat may subsist throughout its life without drinking, deriving water from its food | The kangaroo rat consumes water hourly from desert pools | The kangaroo rat stores water within its tail in the manner of the hump of a camel | The kangaroo rat subsists exclusively upon the blood of other animals
exam: Ascertain the aggregate number of humps borne by four Bactrian camels and one dromedary. | Nine | Five | Eight | Ten
